  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "X3h13tjzgYbF3ptPVenWj4WTULk=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:34:27.525Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The processor documentation carries an explicit \"Not stable yet\" warning that is easy to miss and expensive to discover empirically: **processors run only on the in-process reactor path.** On the SharedWorker reactor path, processor registration is not bridged \u2014 Connect logs a warning and skips every factory (`apps/connect/src/store/reactor.ts`). Worker-hosted reactors do not run processors today.\n\nThe failure mode is the worst kind: silent and total. Nothing throws. Your factory builder is never called, `onOperations` never fires, your relational tables stay empty, and the only signal is a console warning. Anything that depends on a processor \u2014 a search index, an analytics rollup, a webhook, a follow-up dispatch \u2014 simply does not happen.\n\nThis also explains the shape of the registration API. The processor manager is deliberately **not** a method on `IReactor`; it lives on the in-process reactor module as `processorManager`. Reaching it requires narrowing on the module kind, which is the code-level expression of the same constraint:\n\n```typescript\nconst reactorModule =\n  reactorClientModule.kind === \"browser\"\n    ? reactorClientModule.reactorModule\n    : undefined;\n\nawait reactorModule?.processorManager.registerFactory(id, factory);\n```\n\nNote the optional chaining. If the reactor is not on the browser in-process path, `reactorModule` is `undefined` and the `registerFactory` call evaporates without complaint.\n\nPractical consequences while this is unstable:\n\n- Verify which path a host actually booted before debugging a processor that \"does not run\" \u2014 check the module kind, not the processor code.\n- Treat processor-mediated behavior as unavailable in worker-hosted topologies, and do not build UI that assumes a read model will be populated.\n- When you do register, use the package name as the `identifier`; re-registering the same identifier unregisters the prior factory first, so registration is idempotent.\n\nRelated but separate: a factory that *throws* is caught, logged as `Factory '<id>' failed for drive '<driveId>'`, and skipped for that drive \u2014 registration does not crash.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/01-Reactor/05-Processors.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T14:34:26.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:34:27.525Z"
    }
  },
